# Diagnostics for the three editor errors on the consolidated rule.
#
# All three came from Kibana's client-side ES|QL validator (the squiggles in the
# query editor), not from Elasticsearch. Two were real, one is about the editor's
# view of your cluster. Run these to confirm on your side.

# ==========================================================================
# 1. Lines 2 and 7: "invalid input types for ==" / "Invalid input types for AVG"
# ==========================================================================
# Cause: event.dataset and system.cpu.total.norm.pct both resolve to type
# "unsupported" over prod:metricbeat-*, because they are mapped with conflicting
# types across the indices that pattern matches. ES|QL refuses a multi-typed
# field, so == gets (unsupported, keyword) and AVG gets (unsupported).
#
# This is the same root cause as the verification_exception Stage 1 was already
# failing with. That error named 3 legacy 7.x indices "and [3] other indices",
# so excluding metricbeat-7* was never going to be enough -- hence the explicit
# casts in the rule now.
#
# _field_caps is the definitive view. Anything reporting more than one type
# block for a field is a conflict; each block lists the indices involved:

GET prod:metricbeat-*/_field_caps?fields=event.dataset,system.cpu.total.norm.pct,host.hostname,labels.client_code,labels.app_code&filters=-parent

# The rule already casts the two known offenders:
#
#   | EVAL event_dataset = event.dataset::keyword,
#          cpu_pct       = system.cpu.total.norm.pct::double
#   | WHERE event_dataset == "system.cpu" AND cpu_pct IS NOT NULL
#
# Casting is the documented fix for multi-typed fields ("union types"). It works
# as long as the ONLY reference to the raw field is the cast itself -- which is
# why the raw names do not appear anywhere else in the query.
#
# If _field_caps shows host.hostname, labels.client_code or labels.app_code also
# conflicting, cast them the same way BEFORE they are used. host.hostname is the
# join key, so it would become:
#
#   | EVAL hostname.keyword = host.hostname::keyword
#
# (replacing the RENAME), and the STATS BY fields would become:
#
#   | EVAL client_code = labels.client_code::keyword,
#          app_code    = labels.app_code::keyword
#
# ...remembering that the action templates read labels.client_code and
# labels.app_code, so you would need a trailing
# RENAME client_code AS labels.client_code, app_code AS labels.app_code
# to keep the emitted event unchanged.
#
# You may still see a residual WARNING (not error) saying the raw field "cannot
# be retrieved, it is unsupported or not indexed; returning null". That is
# expected and harmless: it refers to the uncast field, which is exactly why the
# cast is there. Errors block, warnings do not.

# ==========================================================================
# 2. Line 4: "kibana_sdp_amdb" is not a valid JOIN index
# ==========================================================================
# THIS ERROR IS ACCURATE. It is not about the local index, and it is not a false
# positive. It is telling you the index is not a lookup index ON prod.
#
# The local index being index.mode: lookup is real and is not in question --
# but for this query it is not the copy that matters, and Kibana deliberately
# ignores it. Traced through Kibana 9.3:
#
#   getJoinIndices(query)
#     -> getRemoteClustersFromESQLQuery(query)         => ["prod"]   (from FROM prod:metricbeat-*)
#     -> GET /internal/esql/autocomplete/join/indices?remoteClusters=prod
#     -> EsqlService.getIndicesByIndexMode("lookup", "prod")
#          resolves index.mode=lookup over ["*", "prod:*"]
#     -> getListOfCCSIndices(["prod"], indices)
#          keeps ONLY entries whose name is "cluster:index" for the listed
#          clusters; entries with no colon -- i.e. every LOCAL index -- are
#          skipped outright.
#
# So once the query reads from a remote cluster, the editor's list of valid JOIN
# indices contains only lookup indices that exist ON THAT REMOTE CLUSTER. The
# local kibana_sdp_amdb is excluded by design, because Elasticsearch would not
# use it either: each cluster joins against its own copy.
#
# Confirm by running this AGAINST prod (not local). Empty response = the index
# does not exist there, which is exactly what the editor is reporting:

GET kibana_sdp_amdb/_settings?filter_path=**.mode      # <-- run on prod

# Fix it either way:
#   - create AND POPULATE it on prod (kibana_sdp_amdb_prod_lookup_index), or
#   - use the ENRICH variant (10008_single_stage_enrich_variant plus
#     kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich_policy), which needs nothing on prod.
#
# For completeness, the local check (expected to return "lookup", and does):

GET kibana_sdp_amdb/_settings?filter_path=**.mode      # <-- run on local

# ==========================================================================
# 3. Trust the server, not the editor
# ==========================================================================
# Kibana's ES|QL validator is a static approximation and has known gaps. One that
# affects the ENRICH variant directly: it does not add a policy's enrich_fields
# to its column list, so it reports Unknown column "group" / "alert_status" after
# the ENRICH even when the policy resolves correctly. Verified against Kibana's
# own test fixtures -- a deliberately wrong policy name produces an additional
# "Unknown policy" error, so policy resolution works while the enrich columns are
# simply never registered.
#
# So: run the query. Test query in the rule editor, or here, is the authority:

POST _query?format=txt
{
  "query": "FROM prod:metricbeat-* | WHERE @timestamp > NOW() - 5 minutes | EVAL event_dataset = event.dataset::keyword, cpu_pct = system.cpu.total.norm.pct::double | WHERE event_dataset == \"system.cpu\" AND cpu_pct IS NOT NULL | STATS avg_cpu = AVG(cpu_pct) * 100 BY host.hostname | SORT avg_cpu DESC | LIMIT 20"
}

# That isolates the metricbeat half with the casts applied and no join, so a
# clean result confirms the fix for errors 1 and 2 independently of the AMDB
# lookup. Add the join or the enrich back once it returns rows.
#
# Then confirm the config filter resolves, on whichever route you picked:

POST _query?format=txt
{
  "query": "FROM prod:metricbeat-* | WHERE @timestamp > NOW() - 5 minutes | EVAL cpu_pct = system.cpu.total.norm.pct::double | WHERE cpu_pct IS NOT NULL | RENAME host.hostname AS hostname.keyword | LOOKUP JOIN kibana_sdp_amdb ON hostname.keyword | MV_EXPAND group | WHERE group == \"10008\" AND alert_status == \"enabled\" | KEEP hostname.keyword, group, alert_status | LIMIT 20"
}

# Zero rows with no error here means the join found no matching config -- check
# the prod copy exists, is in lookup mode, and is populated. Remote clusters
# default to skip_unavailable: true, so a missing index can be reported as a
# skipped cluster rather than an error; check the _clusters section of the
# response (drop ?format=txt to see it).
